### Accounting

xmlconf's `xmlconf.xml` manifest lists 1377 TEST entries across the
sub-suites actually parsed for this vendoring (xmltest, sun-valid,
sun-not-wf, ibm-valid, ibm-not-wf, oasis, eduni namespaces-1.0 + its
errata-1e addendum). Of those, 130 are vendored (25 not-wf "reject" cases +
105 valid "accept" cases); the rest are excluded, each with a specific reason
recorded mechanically as each is classified (see `xmlconf_test.zig`'s module
comment for the categories and `xmlconf_vectors.zig`'s canary test for the
exact counts). In descending order of how much they excluded:

  * DOCTYPE-bearing not-wf case (894 of the pre-xmltest-exclusion total, most
    of which were themselves inside `xmltest`): this module's default policy
    (`doctype = .reject`) rejects ANY `<!DOCTYPE>` before ever looking at what
    is wrong inside it, so a not-wf case whose labeled defect lives in the
    DTD would "pass" trivially without exercising that defect at all.
  * `xmltest` sub-suite (359 entries): license, see above.
  * valid case's DOCTYPE has an external subset (70): no filesystem/network
    access exists in this parser by construction (see README "Hardening").
  * valid case declares a custom GENERAL entity, directly or indirectly via
    parameter-entity expansion (66 + 1): this module never supports
    user-defined entities, by design (the billion-laughs guard) — under
    `doctype = .ignore` the entire internal subset is skipped unparsed, so
    any later reference to a declared-but-unsupported entity fails as
    `UndefinedEntity`. (xmlconf's own `ENTITIES` manifest attribute turned out
    NOT to reliably predict this — several `ENTITIES="none"` cases, e.g.
    `valid-sa-023`, still declare-and-use a custom entity — so this is
    detected directly from each file's own DOCTYPE content, not trusted from
    the manifest attribute.)
  * `type="invalid"` (21): a DTD *validity* constraint — this is a
    non-validating parser by design (see README "Out of scope").
  * `type="error"` (5): xmlconf's own "error" category is explicitly an
    OPTIONAL condition an XML processor "must" not necessarily detect
    (deprecated/ambiguous constructs), not a well-formedness requirement.
  * XML/Namespaces 1.1 cases: this module is XML 1.0 (+ Namespaces 1.0) only.
  * wide/multi-byte-NUL (e.g. UTF-16) encoded cases: UTF-8 only, no
    transcoding (all such cases in this parse happened to be inside the now
    wholly-excluded `xmltest`, so this bucket is empty in the FINAL count, but
    the check remains in the vendoring script for any future re-vendor).
  * one bare-colon-Name case (`xmltest`'s `valid-sa-012`, now moot — already
    covered by the blanket `xmltest` exclusion above, but named individually
    in the vendoring script because it is its OWN scope boundary: mandatory
    Namespaces-in-XML processing rejects a bare `:` as a whole Name, which
    plain (non-namespace) XML 1.0 permits) and one indirect-entity case
    (`sun-valid`'s `v-pe02`, NOT in `xmltest` — excluded on its own merits:
    it builds a general entity declaration indirectly through
    parameter-entity expansion inside the DTD, invisible to both static file
    inspection and this module's design, which never processes parameter
    entities either).

Only test-fixture text (tiny hand-written-looking XML snippets and their
one-line English descriptions, copied verbatim from the sub-suites above) is
reproduced. The implementation under test (`parse` and friends in
`src/root.zig`) is the authors' own (MIT); its design references (W3C XML 1.0
5th ed., Namespaces in XML 1.0 3rd ed., OWASP XXE guidance) are recorded in
`SPEC.md`, not as a condition.
